2020.question:
Does the flight operations inspection
organization ensure that the air operator has
established procedures for the pilot-in-
command to report promptly to air traffic
control (ATC) a suspected communicable
disease, with transmission of the following
information:

a) Aircraft identification;
b) Departure aerodrome;
c) Destination aerodrome;
d) Estimated time of arrival;
e) Number of persons on board;
f) Number of suspected case(s) on
board; and
g) Nature of the public health risk, if
known?

2020.guidance:
1) Review operations inspectors’ procedures to
ensure that they check air operators’ related
procedures.

2) Confirm effective implementation by verifying
that an air operator’s operations manual has
procedures for reporting suspected communicable
diseases, including transmission of the following
information:

a) Aircraft identification;
b) Departure aerodrome;
c) Destination aerodrome;
d) Estimated time of arrival;
e) Number of persons on board;
f) Number of suspected case(s) on board;
and
g) Nature of the public health risk, if known.

Verify that regulations include requirements on:

1) Maintenance organization’s procedures
manual;

2) Safety management system;

3) Maintenance procedures and quality
assurance system;

4) Facilities;

5) Personnel;

6) Completion and retention of records; and

7) Issue of maintenance release.

Review procedures to verify the inclusion of the
following phases:

1) Pre-application;
2) Formal application;

3) Document evaluation;

4) Demonstration and inspection; and

5) Certification.

Verify the following details:

1) certificate number;

2) holder’s name and address;

3) product identification ( e.g. aircraft, engine or
propeller type designation);

4) applicable airworthiness regulations;

5) statement attesting compliance with the applicable
airworthiness requirements;

6) statement incorporating or referencing the type
certificate data sheet; and

7) date of issuance and signature of the issuing
authority.

